so many different angles to look at this  story we've heard from Tyson Foods  talking about a major disruption to our  food supply possible meat shortage  you're looking at the crops angle the  vegetable the produce angle of all this  how has this pandemic affected our food  supply thanks for having me Sandra it's  been very very difficult we have in this  country highly efficient and very safe  food supply infrastructure those supply  chains have been well developed over the  years and when this shutdown happened it  literally blew them up we are trying to  repeat all of our supply chains to be  able to continue providing safe and  affordable and fresh food to consumers  through grocery outlets but the loss of  the retail or the restaurant sector  really threw a grenade in the middle of  our food supply chain so Dave you  Western growers supplies more than half  of the fruits and vegetables in the  United States  farmers your farmers are based in  California Arizona New Mexico and  Colorado so give us an example of how  you have had to just see some of that  food go to waste because essentially you  don't have an end destination for it  last week I was in the desert area of  California and Imperial County in the  southeast corner of the state as one of  my members plowed 350 acres of very high  quality romaine lettuce right back into  the ground there was literally no place  to put that product we've more than  doubled our donations to food banks in  California and still there is so much  fresh produce that of course is highly  perishable and doesn't have a market  because of the loss of the restaurant  and food service sector that these  farmers really have no choice they spend  about half of the total dollar  harvesting the crop if there's no place  to sell that crop you don't you can't  spend that money on harvest and you have  to put it back in the ground and it's  really tragic
 

